00:46:17,030 --> 00:46:21,430
that definition of done, it's a generic definition of done.

679
00:46:21,430 --> 00:46:25,990
That's kind of a catch all for all the different types of work items that they take on.

680
00:46:25,990 --> 00:46:29,910
Maybe in the beginning because they only did it the first time and never revisited it.

681
00:46:29,920 --> 00:46:39,349
It only contains a part of the criteria that they normally would have to take on,

682
00:46:39,349 --> 00:46:41,589
especially as their work evolved over time.

683
00:46:41,589 --> 00:46:45,829
But it's the typically it's the type of thing that is created and then just forgotten.

684
00:46:45,829 --> 00:46:49,590
So in so many cases, in fact the vast majority of cases. when I would work with a team,

685
00:46:49,590 --> 00:46:52,790
I would ask them, so do you have a definition of done?

686
00:46:52,790 --> 00:46:54,630
And half the team wouldn't even know that they had one.

687
00:46:54,640 --> 00:46:57,190
And some people who might have been there from the beginning would say,

688
00:46:57,200 --> 00:47:03,751
"Yeah, we have a definition of done." And I I would ask them, "Well, where is it?" And then the half of those people.

689
00:47:03,760 --> 00:47:06,630
might not even know where it is. And somebody would find out and they say,

690
00:47:06,640 --> 00:47:09,349
"Okay, here's where it is and we'll take a look at it."

691
00:47:09,349 --> 00:47:11,670
And then I would ask them, "Have you.

692
00:47:11,670 --> 00:47:15,910
actually been looking at this definition of done at the end of the sprint.

693
00:47:15,910 --> 00:47:20,790
and making sure that every work item actually adheres to this definition of done?"

694
00:47:20,790 --> 00:47:24,150
And then the answer would usually be no.

695
00:47:24,150 --> 00:47:26,390
And then the question would lead to well why not?

696
00:47:26,400 --> 00:47:33,750
Well because it's because it's not most of those things don't apply to this particular work item.

697
00:47:33,750 --> 00:47:36,470
or something like that or you know we've forgotten.

698
00:47:36,470 --> 00:47:40,950
So the problem with that type of thing is that it's like almost a safety blanket.

699
00:47:40,950 --> 00:47:44,230
We can say that we have it but we don't really use it.

700
00:47:44,230 --> 00:47:48,470
It doesn't really serve a true purpose other than just making us feel warm and fuzzy.

701
00:47:48,470 --> 00:47:52,230
that we've actually created this thing that half the people don't even know exists anymore.

702
00:47:52,230 --> 00:47:58,630
The problem is is that each work item itself is typically different.

703
00:47:58,630 --> 00:48:00,790
Even in teams that do similar work.

704
00:48:00,790 --> 00:48:02,870
and work on the same systems over.

705
00:48:02,870 --> 00:48:06,870
and over and adding maybe making some small changes to the same piece of functionality.

706
00:48:06,870 --> 00:48:14,710
over and over, many cases those work items are different in shape and nature. And so how many requirements do you have?

707
00:48:14,710 --> 00:48:16,550
What are the acceptance criteria in there?

708
00:48:16,550 --> 00:48:19,270
Do you need to create documentation?

709
00:48:19,270 --> 00:48:23,829
Do you truly need to actually go and do a an architectural review?

710
00:48:23,829 --> 00:48:28,790
Is there something that we need to do that goes beyond just this discussion?

711
00:48:28,790 --> 00:48:30,630
Do we need to do something with a support team?

712
00:48:30,630 --> 00:48:35,829
Do we need to have user acceptance done on that?

713
00:48:35,829 --> 00:48:38,950
Do we need to have legal review of that, etc., etc.?

714
00:48:38,950 --> 00:48:44,870
When you take a look at a typical team's backlog of like the last 100 product backlog items.

715
00:48:44,870 --> 00:48:46,630
or stories that they've developed.

716
00:48:46,630 --> 00:48:50,710
and you look across them and you really dig into the details of each one of them,

717
00:48:50,710 --> 00:48:55,990
you'll understand that the true definition of done is so vastly different.

718
00:48:55,990 --> 00:48:59,030
in many cases across that backlog of work items.

719
00:48:59,040 --> 00:49:03,990
that the original definition of done that people have kind of internalized.

720
00:49:03,990 --> 00:49:08,310
or maybe not even remember just doesn't even apply. apply to it.

721
00:49:08,310 --> 00:49:09,910
It might have too many things.

722
00:49:09,910 --> 00:49:12,470
It might not have enough things in it.

723
00:49:12,470 --> 00:49:19,030
And therein lies again the reason why teams end up failing in actually completing the work items.

724
00:49:19,030 --> 00:49:21,990
that they've been asked to implement.

725
00:49:21,990 --> 00:49:25,670
because they don't really understand what done means.

726
00:49:25,670 --> 00:49:27,829
for that particular item.

727
00:49:27,829 --> 00:49:29,671
So that's the one part of it. and the other part of it is.

728
00:49:29,671 --> 00:49:34,150
that you know most of the time scrum teams are asked to estimate the size.

729
00:49:34,150 --> 00:49:37,109
you know the effort for that work item.

730
00:49:37,109 --> 00:49:45,910
and what I've realized over time is that teams don't really succeed in providing a reliable estimate.

731
00:49:45,910 --> 00:49:52,069
if they don't actually understand what done means for that specific work item.

732
00:49:52,069 --> 00:49:55,750
So sitting down with a work item.

733
00:49:55,750 --> 00:50:05,350
then and actually discussing and then eventually codifying you know defining what done really means for this work. item.

734
00:50:05,350 --> 00:50:12,069
gives the team a much greater clarity on what the actual work is going to be.

735
00:50:12,069 --> 00:50:14,630
that they need to go and do not just from an implementation perspective.

736
00:50:14,640 --> 00:50:18,950
but also what other things need to be done as part of this.

737
00:50:18,950 --> 00:50:20,870
Do we need to create documentation?

738
00:50:20,870 --> 00:50:23,191
Do we need to go and have a review with legal etc. Right?

739
00:50:23,200 --> 00:50:27,430
And that creates the type of clarity for a team to truly understand.

740
00:50:27,430 --> 00:50:30,390
and be able to succeed with that specific work item.

741
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:36,470
So it's really about trying to take every single work item.

742
00:50:36,470 --> 00:50:39,030
and treating it as if it is a special case of its own.

743
00:50:39,040 --> 00:50:42,630
and making sure that we have truly captured what done means.

744
00:50:42,630 --> 00:50:44,950
for that work item.

745
00:50:44,950 --> 00:50:49,589
And over time we'll find that there are kind of categories of work items.

746
00:50:49,589 --> 00:50:51,670
that have a similar shape and size in terms of the definition of done.

747
00:50:51,670 --> 00:50:53,349
and what needs to be done.

748
00:50:53,349 --> 00:50:55,349
So not all work is the same, right?

749
00:50:55,349 --> 00:50:57,190
So sometimes there's database work that needs to be done.

750
00:50:57,200 --> 00:51:04,151
Sometimes there's I guess an API that needs to be created that's different from. or making changes to an API.

751
00:51:04,151 --> 00:51:08,309
which is potentially different from making changes to the user interface,

752
00:51:08,309 --> 00:51:10,470
graphical design of that, etc.

753
00:51:10,470 --> 00:51:12,790
And so these types of work items can mean different things.

754
00:51:12,800 --> 00:51:16,069
that need to be done for that work item.

755
00:51:16,079 --> 00:51:19,430
And so as you start seeing that there are these categories.

756
00:51:19,430 --> 00:51:23,589
then you can say okay well this type of work item is about like that.

757
00:51:23,589 --> 00:51:25,829
And so that's what leads us to.

758
00:51:25,829 --> 00:51:29,589
also sometimes creating different templates for definition of done with different type of work items.

759
00:51:29,599 --> 00:51:32,549
In every case, we want to make sure.

760
00:51:32,549 --> 00:51:39,670
that the team has a precise and accurate understanding.

761
00:51:39,670 --> 00:51:41,670
and documentation of what done will mean for that work item.

762
00:51:41,670 --> 00:51:45,750
and not just assume that we understand what it is.

763
00:51:45,750 --> 00:51:51,990
It's like giving them the ability to at the end to certify we have done everything. that we have agreed upon from the beginning.

764
00:51:51,990 --> 00:51:56,230
before we started implementing that needed to have been done.

765
00:51:56,230 --> 00:52:00,150
to be able to call this thing done and say we have implemented it.

766
00:52:00,150 --> 00:52:04,950
and that behavior as well as this you know not just the documentation.

767
00:52:04,950 --> 00:52:09,829
but the the doing of this and certifying of that at the end.

768
00:52:09,829 --> 00:52:13,829
creates a level of trust and acceleration in teams that is unlike.

769
00:52:13,839 --> 00:52:17,589
What I see in teams not doing this.

770
00:52:17,599 --> 00:52:21,589
the challenge is you know you've got various types of programmers.

771
00:52:21,589 --> 00:52:26,390
you've got junior programmers people have just started off.

772
00:52:26,390 --> 00:52:29,910
and you've got programmers who are lazy.

773
00:52:29,910 --> 00:52:33,510
and what stops them then because it's been say an organizational mandate.

774
00:52:33,510 --> 00:52:35,510
or it's a mandate for that particular program.

775
00:52:35,510 --> 00:52:41,270
that you need to have a specific definition of done for each work item.

776
00:52:41,270 --> 00:52:44,470
What stops them from just doing a copy paste for you know.

777
00:52:44,470 --> 00:52:49,349
and not making it specific for that task or that work item?

778
00:52:49,349 --> 00:52:55,589
What do you look for in that definition of done to ensure it's a good quality one.

779
00:52:55,589 --> 00:52:57,589
which will ensure that it is can be seen as sign off.

780
00:52:57,599 --> 00:53:02,150
So it comes down to responsibility in my opinion.

781
00:53:02,150 --> 00:53:05,589
The definition of done isn't just signed off by one party.

782
00:53:05,589 --> 00:53:14,790
The definition of done has to be approved by everyone and everyone approving it becomes responsible for it.

783
00:53:14,790 --> 00:53:19,349
So the agreement manifest in the definition of done is.

784
00:53:19,349 --> 00:53:22,870
that when all of these items are truly met,

785
00:53:22,870 --> 00:53:28,549
this work item will close and responsibility for it will transfer from the team.

786
00:53:28,549 --> 00:53:31,349
back to you know whoever is in charge of product, product owner, product manager, whatever, right?

787
00:53:31,349 --> 00:53:37,990
The product manager then has an impetus to ensure that there are no,

788
00:53:37,990 --> 00:53:40,309
for lack of a better term, loopholes, right?

789
00:53:40,309 --> 00:53:45,589
That that definition of done is the right definition of done for that work item.

790
00:53:45,599 --> 00:53:47,349
So that when it comes back to them,

791
00:53:47,349 --> 00:53:51,270
they don't get to point the figure finger at the team and say they did it wrong.

792
00:53:51,280 --> 00:53:55,030
because the definition of done is what the team was supposed to do.

793
00:53:55,030 --> 00:53:56,710
and the team did the definition of done, right?

794
00:53:56,710 --> 00:54:01,589
So once they've accepted it, if something's wrong,

795
00:54:01,589 --> 00:54:03,589
they're going to be the ones that are on the hook for being wrong.

796
00:54:03,589 --> 00:54:09,990
So they're going to push back on accepting it on the basis of that definition of done.

797
00:54:09,990 --> 00:54:12,230
But they can't push back in this process.

798
00:54:12,230 --> 00:54:17,990
They can't push back on the basis of like, oh, I forgot something or whatever.

799
00:54:17,990 --> 00:54:23,670
So they've got a a big driver to make sure that definition of done is right.

800
00:54:23,670 --> 00:54:26,069
The team has a similar driver, right?

801
00:54:26,069 --> 00:54:29,190
They want to make sure that the definition of done is exactly right.

802
00:54:29,190 --> 00:54:36,309
so that they can use it to implement things in a way.

803
00:54:36,309 --> 00:54:39,670
that will get the work item to be accepted and closed and move forward.

804
00:54:39,680 --> 00:54:44,069
And they're going to have some pressure if if they're really lazy,

805
00:54:44,069 --> 00:54:47,990
which I I mean I don't know how many teams are really lazy, but if there is a team that's really lazy,

806
00:54:47,990 --> 00:54:53,829
they'll have pressure from outside that says, "Well, you've also got to have the architecture review.

807
00:54:53,829 --> 00:54:55,510
You've got to have all these things, right?

808
00:54:55,510 --> 00:55:00,391
You've got to have all of these standards be met in order for this work item to be. closed.

809
00:55:00,391 --> 00:55:04,630
And that way we keep our code quality higher and we can maintain the product over time.

810
00:55:04,630 --> 00:55:08,630
So, it's the tension between how do I get work started on it?

811
00:55:08,630 --> 00:55:13,750
Well, getting work started on it means everybody's agreed on a work item.

812
00:55:13,750 --> 00:55:16,151
or on the definition of done. So, that's one pressure.

813
00:55:16,151 --> 00:55:20,951
And that pressure makes it so that people don't try to like throw everything in the kitchen sink. in it.

814
00:55:20,951 --> 00:55:22,870
They try to make it just right for just that item.

815
00:55:22,870 --> 00:55:26,710
And then the other pressure is how are we going to get this handed off at the end.

816
00:55:26,710 --> 00:55:36,231
And that makes sure that everybody has what they need in order to be able to later be. held to account.

817
00:55:36,231 --> 00:55:39,190
and kind of pass that test for that work item in it.

818
00:55:39,190 --> 00:55:42,790
It's the tension between those two forces, right?

819
00:55:42,790 --> 00:55:47,030
And this transfer of responsibility is what stops product owners saying.

820
00:55:47,030 --> 00:55:51,349
that you know I thought this was an implicit requirement in the early example you had given.

821
00:55:51,349 --> 00:55:53,910
This is where it you know gets caught.

822
00:55:53,910 --> 00:55:57,030
Nothing actually stops them from saying it right.

823
00:55:57,030 --> 00:55:59,349
All you can do is create consequences when they do say it.

824
00:55:59,349 --> 00:56:04,150
So when the team has an agreement and presumably management is in on this agreement.

825
00:56:04,150 --> 00:56:06,150
This isn't something you can do in a really grassroots way.

826
00:56:06,150 --> 00:56:08,069
You've got to have management involved.

827
00:56:08,069 --> 00:56:15,671
You might even need leadership involved and people from other parts of the organization other than engineering involved. and on board.

828
00:56:15,671 --> 00:56:20,549
Once you have that kind of alignment and agreement, this is how we're going to do business.

829
00:56:20,559 --> 00:56:27,109
Then I guess if a product owner were to say, oh, I forgot this piece, so we need to do more.

830
00:56:27,109 --> 00:56:31,430
It's the team's responsibility to say that's not how this works.

831
00:56:31,430 --> 00:56:33,109
And then you know I guess if the product owner is really stubborn.

832
00:56:33,109 --> 00:56:34,870
and holding it open or whatever.

833
00:56:34,870 --> 00:56:36,630
then there'd be some sort of escalation process.

834
00:56:36,630 --> 00:56:39,109
where you get intervention from management.

835
00:56:39,109 --> 00:56:44,710
I really don't find that to be a very common problem. because I mean initially like in the training phase it might be.

836
00:56:44,720 --> 00:56:46,549
but everybody wants to do a good job.

837
00:56:46,559 --> 00:56:51,430
Everybody wants things to go smoothly and this makes things go smoothly and gets us a better job.

838
00:56:51,440 --> 00:56:54,309
Right. Got it. Lunal you had something to add.

839
00:56:54,319 --> 00:56:56,870
Yes.

840
00:56:56,870 --> 00:57:01,589
So like Max actually just said now is most of the times people are trying to do the.

841
00:57:01,589 --> 00:57:05,430
right thing but what this gives us is two things.

842
00:57:05,430 --> 00:57:12,309
One so in addition to helping us solve a dysfunctional environment.

843
00:57:12,309 --> 00:57:18,230
where people are not taking responsibility or they're being lazy.

844
00:57:18,230 --> 00:57:22,150
or they have poor practices or something like that.

845
00:57:22,150 --> 00:57:27,270
It also helps in scenarios where teams are truly highly successful and highly capable,

846
00:57:27,270 --> 00:57:31,430
highly motivated but just want to make sure that the right thing gets done.

847
00:57:31,430 --> 00:57:34,390
And one of the examples that I like to.

848
00:57:34,390 --> 00:57:39,270
or one of the things I guess mechanisms I want to talk about is that in scrum.

849
00:57:39,270 --> 00:57:45,990
a good product owner understands that they are ultimately responsible.

850
00:57:45,990 --> 00:57:48,630
for what the scrum team develops.

851
00:57:48,630 --> 00:57:53,190
They have a a fiduciary responsibility to the organization to essentially put the.

852
00:57:53,200 --> 00:57:56,230
right things into the team's sprints,

853
00:57:56,240 --> 00:58:03,030
the right work items, and make sure that the team ultimately is producing the value.

854
00:58:03,030 --> 00:58:05,030
that the company is paying for, right?

855
00:58:05,030 --> 00:58:07,510
Because the company is paying a certain amount per sprint, if you will.

856
00:58:07,520 --> 00:58:10,870
And that goes, you know, for how much they're paying for the facilities,

857
00:58:10,870 --> 00:58:14,230
but they're paying for salaries of the people involved,

858
00:58:14,230 --> 00:58:15,910
the product owner, the scrum master, the scrum team, etc.

859
00:58:15,920 --> 00:58:18,230
Right?

860
00:58:18,230 --> 00:58:21,430
And so as the product owner, you are kind of like a mini CEO almost.

861
00:58:21,430 --> 00:58:27,750
You have this fiduciary responsibility to make sure that the team is working on the right things.

862
00:58:27,750 --> 00:58:30,230
and are producing the right things to create the value.

863
00:58:30,230 --> 00:58:32,309
that the company is paying for.

864
00:58:32,309 --> 00:58:37,670
So as a product owner myself, I want to make sure.

865
00:58:37,670 --> 00:58:40,470
that I am giving the team the best chance.

866
00:58:40,470 --> 00:58:42,309
of succeeding in delivering this thing that I'm asking them to deliver.

867
00:58:42,319 --> 00:58:47,990
So having clarity on the definition of done,

868
00:58:47,990 --> 00:58:50,549
what it actually means to be done is not just important for the team.

869
00:58:50,549 --> 00:58:55,030
It's important for me because I need to make sure.

870
00:58:55,030 --> 00:58:58,230
that the team has this clear understanding of what done actually means.

871
00:58:58,230 --> 00:59:01,589
But it also means I have to have a clear understanding of what done means.

872
00:59:01,589 --> 00:59:06,150
And if I didn't think about all these other things that needed to be done,

873
00:59:06,150 --> 00:59:07,750
then I I might be setting the team up for failure.

874
00:59:07,750 --> 00:59:11,190
If I don't get an opportunity for the team to point out the additional things.

875
00:59:11,190 --> 00:59:13,030
that need to be done, I'm setting them up for failure.

876
00:59:13,040 --> 00:59:19,349
If I don't give them the ability to actually create a.

877
00:59:19,349 --> 00:59:21,109
or to apply a reliable estimate for instance,

878
00:59:21,109 --> 00:59:26,230
for the work of what they think is going to take in this sprint.

879
00:59:26,230 --> 00:59:28,870
and one that they can feel absolutely that they can stand by.

880
00:59:28,870 --> 00:59:32,069
and that they comfortable with then I'm again I'm setting them up for failure.

881
00:59:32,079 --> 00:59:38,710
So it works both ways and so yes this handoff of responsibility is an absolute forcing function.

882
00:59:38,710 --> 00:59:41,430
to help solve problems when we have dysfunction in that.

883
00:59:41,430 --> 00:59:45,030
but it also helps us create the guard rails.

884
00:59:45,040 --> 00:59:50,950
that even a high performing team needs to have.

885
00:59:50,950 --> 00:59:53,349
to make sure that what's being asked for is clear.

886
00:59:53,349 --> 00:59:55,270
that both parties agree to it.

887
00:59:55,270 --> 00:59:57,190
and that we can actually succeed in delivering that at the end.

888
00:59:57,200 --> 01:00:01,190
Would you be able to explain what the definition of ready is.

889
01:00:01,190 --> 01:00:03,190
and why it needs to be bespoke?

890
01:00:03,200 --> 01:00:12,069
It's similar to the definition of done wherein you know each work item is different.

891
01:00:12,069 --> 01:00:16,950
or should be treated as potentially different from other work items.

892
01:00:16,950 --> 01:00:21,990
But an example of definition of ready we we typically have two categories in our definition of ready.

893
01:00:22,000 --> 01:00:25,109
and that is product exit criteria.

894
01:00:25,109 --> 01:00:32,710
In other words, what are the criteria that needs to be met in order for product management,

895
01:00:32,710 --> 01:00:36,950
your product owner, product manager.

896
01:00:36,950 --> 01:00:39,190
to be able to say this work item and everything in it, the requirements,

897
01:00:39,200 --> 01:00:43,190
you know, the specifications that are listed in there.

898
01:00:43,190 --> 01:00:49,510
and everything that is that is needed to be provided to the team are ready.

899
01:00:49,510 --> 01:00:52,150
So, you know, everything is ready.

900
01:00:52,150 --> 01:00:54,549
So, an example would be not just the requirements itself.

901
01:00:54,549 --> 01:00:56,309
So, if I was to do behavior-driven development,

902
01:00:56,319 --> 01:01:01,589
there would be behavioral specifications for all of the different scenarios.

903
01:01:01,589 --> 01:01:04,309
that are in scope for this work item.

